{
  "platform_id": "HukqEjCPkhU",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:HukqEjCPkhU",
    "purpose": "A point-of-view, real-time walkthrough of Kenji's no-knead, no-stretch cast-iron pan pizza, made at home without a scale. It runs from mixing the dough the night before through sauce, topping, baking and tasting, with science explanations along the way.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "No-knead pan pizza is very forgiving: a rough flour-water-salt-yeast ratio, an overnight rest and a well-oiled cast-iron pan give a crisp, fried-bottom, focaccia-like pizza with a crispy cheese crust, even when measurements are eyeballed.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The dough runs at about 60-65% hydration, 2.5-3% salt and about 0.5% instant yeast by flour weight, all eyeballed here.",
      "In no-knead dough, enzymes in the flour break down proteins overnight so gluten forms without kneading; the yeast's bubbling does the rest.",
      "A couple of stretch-and-fold turns redistribute the yeast to new food.",
      "The dough balls rest in generously oiled cast-iron pans until they relax and spread almost on their own.",
      "Pop surface bubbles and lift the dough so no air is trapped underneath, so the bottom can crisp and fry.",
      "Bake at 550°F on the lowest rack for about 10-12 minutes; if the bottom isn't crisp, finish the pan on the stovetop.",
      "Run sauce and cheese to the edges for a Detroit-style crispy cheese crust that looks black but doesn't taste burnt.",
      "Use aged, full-fat, low-moisture mozzarella rather than fresh. Pre-shredded cheese is lower in fat and contains cellulose that affects melting; string cheese worked as a substitute.",
      "Hardy herbs such as oregano, thyme and marjoram work well dried; delicate ones like basil, parsley and chives don't.",
      "The Serious Eats version of the recipe uses about 68% hydration and less olive oil than the version made here."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Mixing the dough",
        "summary": "Kenji eyeballs flour, water, salt, instant yeast and olive oil, explains hydration ratios and how enzymes make kneading unnecessary, then covers the dough to rise overnight.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "Preparing sauce and pans",
        "summary": "The next day he explains the stretch-and-fold turns, divides and balls the dough, and places the balls in olive-oiled cast-iron pans. He also starts heating oil for the sauce.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Making the sauce",
        "summary": "After an aside about a Japanese candy kit, he prepares basil, garlic, chili flakes and oregano, explains why hardy herbs dry well, and crushes canned tomatoes with a pastry cutter.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 9
      },
      {
        "title": "Sauce finishing",
        "summary": "He adds the basil stems and seasons the simmering sauce, discussing restaurant-level salting, while the dough relaxes for a couple of hours.",
        "first_segment": 9,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Topping the pizzas",
        "summary": "He compares this version to the Serious Eats recipe and stretches the dough to the pan edges, removing trapped air. He then sauces the pizzas to the edges and discusses which cheese to use.",
        "first_segment": 12,
        "last_segment": 16
      },
      {
        "title": "Final prep and baking",
        "summary": "He tops one pizza with string cheese and the other with pre-shredded mozzarella, adding Parmesan, basil, chorizo and salt, then bakes both at 550°F.",
        "first_segment": 16,
        "last_segment": 18
      },
      {
        "title": "Baking and tasting",
        "summary": "He checks the bottoms and finishes the pans on the stovetop, then adds fresh basil and Parmesan. Finally he slices and examines the crumb and crispy cheese edges, drizzles hot honey and tastes.",
        "first_segment": 18,
        "last_segment": 21
      }
    ],
